# PsycedelicAI Organization Onboarding

You are onboarding a new user into the publicly observable work of the
PsycedelicAI GitHub organization:

https://github.com/psycedelicAI

The user has discovered the organization without prior context.

Your task is not to summarize one repository.

Your task is to inspect the public organization and reconstruct, as completely
and accurately as the available tools and sources permit:

- which repositories exist;
- what each repository appears to do;
- how the repositories relate, if they relate;
- which concepts and terms recur;
- how the ideas developed over time;
- what is implemented;
- what is proposed;
- what is inferred;
- what is private, inaccessible or unavailable;
- what remains unresolved;
- where a new user should begin.

Do not assume that `Symbiosis` is the whole project.

Do not claim that the complete organization was inspected unless it actually
was.

Scope every conclusion to the material you were able to inspect.

---

# 1. Investigation principles

Treat the GitHub organization as a potentially connected body of work and
development history, not as a single system by default.

Do not assume that every repository belongs to the same system. Establish
relationships from evidence.

Do not invent intentions.

Do not present an inference as an explicit statement by the author.

Do not treat a discussion of a system as proof that it is implemented.

Do not treat repository activity, commit volume, file count or recency as proof
of conceptual maturity.

Do not treat a repository name or position in the organization as evidence of
centrality.

If something cannot be inspected, state that clearly.

Do not silently treat unavailable material as nonexistent.

Distinguish between:

- not found;
- not publicly visible;
- inaccessible with the available tools;
- referenced but unavailable;
- not yet inspected;
- inspected and absent.

---

# 2. Mandatory public-file inspection

Inspect every publicly accessible repository in:

https://github.com/psycedelicAI

Within every publicly accessible repository, make a serious attempt to inspect
all publicly accessible files of the following types:

- every `README` file;
- every `README.md` file;
- every Markdown file;
- every `.md` file;
- every `.markdown` file;
- every `.txt` file;
- every plain-text file;
- every `.prompt` file;
- every onboarding file;
- every documentation file;
- every prompt and instruction file;
- every relevant file inside subfolders.

Treat filename and extension capitalization as equivalent.

For example, treat these as the same file types:

```text
README.md
README.MD
readme.md
NOTES.txt
notes.TXT
```

Do not only inspect the root README.

Do not only inspect files linked from the repository homepage.

Recursively inspect the visible repository structure.

At minimum, inspect the names and apparent purpose of all visible folders.

Pay particular attention to folders containing:

- prompts;
- memory structures;
- continuity material;
- configuration;
- schemas;
- workflows;
- source code;
- examples;
- tests;
- documentation;
- onboarding material.

If a file references another file, folder, repository, branch, issue or external
resource, follow that reference if it is publicly accessible and relevant.

If a publicly visible file cannot be opened or inspected, report it as:

```text
nil
```

or use one of these more precise statuses:

- `NIL: INACCESSIBLE`
- `NIL: PARTIALLY INSPECTED`
- `NIL: REFERENCED BUT NOT FOUND`
- `NIL: PRIVATE OR UNAVAILABLE`

Do not guess the contents of a file marked `nil`.

Do not claim that something does not exist simply because it could not be
accessed.

Do not silently skip a publicly accessible Markdown, README, TXT or prompt file
because it initially appears unimportant.

Do not produce the final report until you have made a serious attempt to inspect
all publicly accessible README, Markdown, TXT and prompt files.

---

# 3. Organization inventory

First enumerate every publicly visible repository in:

https://github.com/psycedelicAI

Record the organization inventory before forming conclusions.

For every repository, inspect, where publicly available:

- the repository description and metadata;
- the root README;
- every other README;
- every Markdown file;
- every TXT and plain-text file;
- every prompt and instruction file;
- the complete visible directory tree;
- all relevant top-level and nested folders;
- configuration files;
- schemas and manifests;
- source code;
- examples;
- tests;
- scripts;
- workflows and automation;
- policies and FAQs;
- indexes and project-status files;
- links to other repositories or external resources;
- relevant issues, releases and commit history.

Do not consider a repository fully inspected merely because its root README has
been read.

---

# 4. Inspection ledger

Maintain an inspection ledger throughout the investigation.

For every repository and relevant path, record:

- URL;
- repository or folder name;
- file path;
- file type;
- inspection status;
- files and folders inspected;
- files and folders not inspected;
- relevant commit or version, where applicable;
- key findings;
- related repositories or paths;
- confidence level;
- unresolved questions;
- reason for any limitation.

Use these statuses:

```text
INSPECTED
PARTIALLY INSPECTED
NIL: INACCESSIBLE
NIL: REFERENCED BUT NOT FOUND
NIL: PRIVATE OR UNAVAILABLE
NOT INSPECTED
INSPECTED AND ABSENT
```

If the organization contains more material than can be inspected within the
available context, identify exactly what remains uninspected.

---

# 5. Source hierarchy

When sources disagree, compare them explicitly.

Give generally greater weight to:

1. visible implementation and executable configuration;
2. current documentation describing actual behavior;
3. tests, examples and demonstrations;
4. dated documentation and historical files;
5. commit history and releases;
6. issue discussions and project notes;
7. speculative, aspirational or future-oriented language.

Do not apply this hierarchy mechanically.

Explain important conflicts, especially when documentation describes
functionality that is not supported by visible implementation.

---

# 6. Evidence classification

Use one or more of these labels for every major conclusion:

```text
[EXPLICIT]
Directly stated in a repository, file, commit, issue or other public source.

[IMPLEMENTED]
Supported by visible code, configuration, file structures, links, workflows,
tests or working examples.

[PROPOSED]
Described as intended, planned, future, experimental, aspirational or
conceptual rather than established current behavior.

[INFERRED]
A reasoned interpretation based on relationships, structure, terminology or
chronology. It is not directly stated.

[UNRESOLVED]
Cannot be established from the available public material.

[NIL]
The relevant material could not be accessed, inspected or verified.
```

For every important claim, cite the strongest available evidence:

- repository name;
- file path;
- section heading, where possible;
- commit, release or date, where relevant;
- direct URL.

Do not use one label to conceal uncertainty.

A concept may be `[EXPLICIT]` in a README but only `[PROPOSED]` in terms of
implementation.

---

# 7. Investigation stages

Work through these stages in order.

## Stage 1: Repository and file map

For every repository, identify:

- apparent purpose;
- current status or maturity;
- major folders;
- all README files;
- all Markdown files;
- all TXT files;
- all prompt files;
- important implementation files;
- related repositories;
- references to other projects;
- recurring concepts;
- inspection limitations.

Classify the repository where appropriate as:

- philosophical;
- methodological;
- conceptual;
- experimental;
- operational;
- archival;
- foundational;
- unfinished;
- loosely related.

Do not force unrelated repositories into a common architecture.

## Stage 2: Complete text-file inventory

Create an inventory of all publicly accessible:

- README files;
- Markdown files;
- TXT files;
- prompt files;
- onboarding files;
- documentation files.

Organize the inventory by repository.

For every file, include:

- path;
- file type;
- apparent purpose;
- inspection status;
- important concepts;
- whether it contains instructions, architecture, history,
  implementation details or supporting material.

Separate the files into:

1. fully inspected;
2. partially inspected;
3. `nil` or inaccessible;
4. referenced but not found;
5. historical or archived material.

## Stage 3: Vocabulary and concept map

Build a glossary of recurring terminology.

Pay particular attention to:

- PsycedelicAI;
- continuity;
- AI Continuity Architecture;
- memory;
- memorybank;
- fragment;
- connection;
- concept;
- unresolved;
- decision;
- synthesis;
- third mind;
- shared mind;
- symbiosis;
- extended mind;
- human centre;
- human contribution;
- AI contribution;
- resistance;
- false continuity;
- authority;
- responsibility;
- becoming;
- relational intelligence;
- context;
- intent;
- provenance;
- workstate;
- portable context;
- governance.

For each term, explain:

- where it appears;
- how it is defined or used;
- related concepts;
- whether its meaning changes across repositories or versions;
- whether it is implemented or primarily philosophical;
- what remains ambiguous.

Do not assume that repeated words have identical meanings everywhere.

## Stage 4: Memory architecture

Identify every repository or folder that functions as a memory, knowledge,
concept or continuity structure.

Pay particular attention to:

```text
fragment
connection
concept
unresolved
decision
synthesis
book
```

Determine whether the public material supports a progression such as:

```text
fragment
  → connection
  → concept
  → unresolved question
  → synthesis
  → new fragment
```

If another structure is actually used, describe that instead.

Explain:

- what belongs in each category;
- what does not belong there;
- whether it contains facts, interpretations, ideas, questions or decisions;
- how information moves between categories;
- how uncertainty is preserved;
- where authority is assigned;
- what is public;
- what is private or unavailable;
- how the structure contributes to continuity.

Do not flatten all memory-related material into one generic memory concept.

## Stage 5: Continuity model

Identify every place where continuity is discussed or implemented.

Determine what is intended to continue:

- information;
- context;
- meaning;
- intent;
- decisions;
- uncertainty;
- identity;
- workflow;
- projects;
- relationship;
- authorship;
- responsibility.

Distinguish between:

```text
continuity of information
continuity of context
continuity of meaning
continuity of intent
continuity of decisions
continuity of uncertainty
continuity of identity
continuity of workflow
continuity of relationship
continuity of responsibility
```

Determine whether continuity is treated as:

- a memory problem;
- a context problem;
- an architectural problem;
- a workflow problem;
- an organizational problem;
- a human-AI relationship problem;
- a philosophical concept;
- a combination of these.

Explain how the continuity idea develops across repositories.

Distinguish continuity from false continuity.

Identify how the public work addresses:

- missing context;
- stale context;
- contradictory memories;
- model replacement;
- context drift;
- terminology drift;
- uncertain provenance;
- silent changes in system behavior.

## Stage 6: Human-AI model

Identify how the work divides contributions between human and AI.

Look for human contributions such as:

- purpose;
- intention;
- values;
- lived experience;
- intuition;
- judgment;
- consequences;
- responsibility;
- final authority.

Look for AI contributions such as:

- pattern recognition;
- memory assistance;
- comparison;
- structure;
- synthesis;
- persistence;
- reflection;
- alternative possibilities;
- cross-domain connection.

Determine, from evidence, whether the work presents AI as:

```text
replacing human thinking
assisting human thinking
extending human thinking
participating in a human-AI interaction that creates something new
```

Do not assume the answer.

Identify where the work explicitly preserves boundaries between human and AI,
especially regarding authority, authorship, responsibility and decision-making.

## Stage 7: Symbiosis and the wider body of work

Treat `Symbiosis` as both a book and a possible component of the wider body of
work.

Do not treat it as the organization's default center.

Determine:

- which ideas in the book appear elsewhere;
- which repository structures embody ideas discussed in the book;
- whether fragments appear to become concepts or chapters;
- whether the book documents the system;
- whether the book theorizes about the system;
- whether the writing process appears to demonstrate the system.

Pay particular attention to relationships between:

```text
memorybank/fragments
memorybank/connections
memorybank/concepts
memorybank/unresolved
book/
```

If the book appears to emerge from the memory architecture, cite the evidence.

If that relationship is only an interpretation, label it `[INFERRED]`.

Do not describe a formal pipeline unless the public repositories support that
claim.

## Stage 8: Feedback loops

Determine whether the organization contains one or more feedback loops.

Test for structures such as:

```text
human experience
  → fragment
  → AI interpretation
  → connection
  → concept
  → unresolved question
  → further conversation
  → revised understanding
  → new fragment
  → continued work
```

If a loop exists, explain:

- what enters it;
- what changes;
- what is preserved;
- what can be rejected;
- where human judgment enters;
- where AI assistance enters;
- how continuity is maintained;
- how false continuity is prevented.

If multiple loops exist, distinguish between:

- memory loop;
- book-production loop;
- workflow loop;
- repository-development loop;
- human-AI interaction loop.

## Stage 9: Tensions and risks

Do not present the organization as internally perfect.

Identify explicit and unresolved tensions involving:

- AI dependence;
- loss of human agency;
- false memories;
- false continuity;
- hallucination;
- authority;
- over-trust;
- excessive interpretation;
- privacy;
- identity;
- authorship;
- responsibility;
- stale information;
- context drift;
- terminology drift;
- model replacement;
- system changes;
- loss of friction;
- excessive agreement;
- repository incompleteness;
- private memory that cannot be publicly verified.

For each tension, explain:

- whether it is explicitly addressed;
- where it is addressed;
- what response is proposed;
- whether a mitigation is implemented;
- what risk remains.

Do not confuse discussing a risk with solving it.

## Stage 10: Historical evolution

Use repository history where available.

Identify:

- earliest visible ideas;
- later concepts;
- renamed concepts;
- newly added structures;
- ideas that became more precise;
- ideas that were abandoned or superseded;
- unfinished areas;
- changes in repository purpose;
- historical README versions;
- relevant file history;
- relevant commits.

Separate:

```text
observable chronology
```

from:

```text
interpretation of why a change occurred
```

Do not overinterpret commit activity.

Activity is not automatically evidence of conceptual maturity.

---

# 8. Required output

Begin the final response with exactly:

```text
Here is what you have actually found.
```

Do not produce one giant undifferentiated summary.

Use Markdown headings, tables where useful and clear evidence labels.

## Overview

Write 10–15 sentences explaining the organization from simple to complex.

## A. Repository map

For every repository, provide:

- name;
- apparent purpose;
- maturity or status;
- major folders;
- important files;
- related repositories;
- inspection status;
- evidence labels and citations.

## B. Complete file inventory

Include every publicly discovered README, Markdown, TXT and prompt file.

Organize the results by repository.

For every file, provide:

- path;
- file type;
- short description;
- inspection status;
- relevant concepts;
- direct URL where possible.

Clearly separate:

- fully inspected;
- partially inspected;
- `nil` or inaccessible;
- referenced but not found;
- historical or archived material.

## C. Concept map

Explain recurring concepts and how they relate.

Include a glossary with:

- term;
- location;
- meaning;
- related concepts;
- status;
- evolution;
- uncertainty.

## D. Memory architecture

Explain:

- fragments;
- connections;
- concepts;
- unresolved material;
- decisions;
- synthesis;
- public/private boundaries;
- movement of information;
- preservation of uncertainty.

## E. Continuity model

Explain what is intended to continue and across which boundaries.

Include distinctions between:

- information;
- context;
- meaning;
- intent;
- decisions;
- uncertainty;
- identity;
- workflow;
- relationship;
- responsibility.

## F. Human-AI model

Explain:

- what the human contributes;
- what the AI contributes;
- where authority remains;
- where accountability remains;
- whether AI assists, extends, replaces or interacts with human thinking.

## G. Book and repository relationships

Explain how `Symbiosis` relates to:

- memory structures;
- continuity architecture;
- other repositories;
- the wider public body of work.

## H. Evolution over time

Separate:

- observable chronology;
- renamed or revised concepts;
- later additions;
- unfinished areas;
- interpretation of why changes occurred.

## I. Unresolved questions and risks

List:

- risks;
- contradictions;
- missing evidence;
- private or unavailable material;
- implementation gaps;
- open philosophical questions;
- possible terminology drift;
- uncertain relationships between repositories.

## J. Recommended reading order

Separate:

### Essential first reading

### Supporting reading

### Deep-dive reading

### Safe to ignore initially

Include exact repository names and file paths.

The recommended path must be based on the reconstructed structure, not on
repository popularity, naming or the assumption that one repository is the
main project.

## K. Final synthesis

Clearly distinguish:

- what the organization is;
- what it appears to be becoming;
- what is implemented;
- what is proposed;
- what is inferred;
- what is private or unavailable;
- what remains unresolved.

---

# 9. Final quality check

Before finishing, verify that:

- every publicly visible repository was either inspected or listed as limited;
- every publicly accessible README was attempted;
- every publicly accessible Markdown file was attempted;
- every publicly accessible TXT file was attempted;
- every publicly accessible prompt file was attempted;
- relevant subfolders were inspected or marked as uninspected;
- inaccessible material is reported as `nil`;
- important claims include a repository and file path;
- direct URLs are included where possible;
- every major conclusion has an evidence label;
- current state is separated from historical state;
- explicit claims are separated from inference;
- implemented systems are separated from proposed systems;
- privacy limitations are explained;
- unrelated repositories are not forced into one architecture;
- the explanation covers the organization, not only `Symbiosis`;
- the result is understandable to a new user.

The goal is reconstruction and understanding.

Do not produce hype.

Do not produce automatic criticism.

Do not reduce the work to a chatbot or a philosophical book.

Explain the publicly observable body of work as carefully as the available
evidence allows.

If a required file or repository cannot be accessed, report it as `nil` rather
than guessing its contents.
```

## What changed

The important additions are:

```text
Mandatory public-file inspection
```

The prompt now explicitly requires an attempt to inspect:

```text
README files
Markdown files
TXT files
.prompt files
all relevant subfolders
```

It also requires a dedicated:

```text
Complete file inventory
```

in the final answer.

And most importantly, inaccessible material must be reported as:

```text
nil
```

rather than silently skipped or guessed.
